// In-depth battles, batch d: gaixia, pidna, mobei, carras, alesia, farsalia.
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "legião": "The main unit of the Roman army, with about 4,000 to 5,000 heavy infantry soldiers at the end of the Republic, divided into cohorts and centuries.",
  "coorte": "A subdivision of the legion, with about 480 men. A legion had ten cohorts, which made it possible to detach pieces of it for different missions.",
  "manípulo": "A small unit of the early legion, of 120 to 160 men. It was more flexible than the phalanx and could advance over rough terrain.",
  "gládio": "The short Roman sword, about 60 cm long, made for stabbing at close range, protected by the shield.",
  "pilo": "The heavy javelin of the Roman legionary, thrown just before the clash. The iron tip bent and stuck in the enemy shield.",
  "arqueiro a cavalo": "A horseman who shoots arrows from the saddle, even on the move. It was the typical weapon of the steppe peoples, from the Parthians to the Mongols.",
  "catafratários": "Extremely heavy cavalry, with both rider and horse covered in scale or mail armor. They charged with a long lance.",
  "tiro parta": "Pretending to flee at a gallop and, turned in the saddle, shooting arrows backward at those who pursue. It gave rise to the English expression 'parting shot'.",
  "testudo": "The 'tortoise': legionaries packed tight, with shields in front and over their heads, like a shell. It protected against arrows, but made the troops slow.",
  "circunvalação": "A line of fortification built around a besieged city, facing inward, so that no one gets out.",
  "contravalação": "A second line of fortification, facing outward, that protects the besiegers from an army coming to relieve the city. (The authors sometimes swap the two names.)",
  "triunfo": "A solemn parade in Rome in honor of a victorious general, with the booty and the illustrious prisoners, who were often executed at the end.",
  "chanyu": "Title of the supreme leader of the Xiongnu confederation.",
  "xiongnu": "A confederation of nomadic horse peoples that dominated the steppes north of China between the 3rd century BC and the 1st century AD.",
  "rei hegemônico": "Xiang Yu proclaimed himself 'Hegemon-King of Western Chu': a king above the other kings, without being an emperor."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  gaixia: {
    war: "Chu–Han War (206–202 BC)",
    hook: "The night a song defeated the greatest warrior of China.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 221 BC, the kingdom of Qin unified China by force. Its first emperor standardized writing, coinage and measures, and governed with very harsh laws. When he died, in 210 BC, the empire exploded in revolts. By 206 BC, the Qin dynasty was over.",
        "Two men were left who were capable of taking everything. Xiang Yu, from a family of generals of the old kingdom of Chu, divided China into eighteen kingdoms and made himself [[Hegemon-King|rei hegemônico]] above them. Liu Bang, a former village official, received the small kingdom of Han, in the west, which was almost an exile. He did not accept it, and the war between the two lasted four years.",
        { h: "The road to Gaixia" },
        { timeline: [
          ["206 BC", "Fall of Qin. Xiang Yu parcels out the empire; Liu Bang becomes king of Han."],
          ["205 BC", "Liu Bang takes the Chu capital, Pengcheng, and is crushed by Xiang Yu in a counterattack."],
          ["204–203 BC", "While Liu Bang holds out at Xingyang and Chenggao, his general Han Xin conquers the north and the east."],
          ["203 BC", "Treaty of the Honggou canal: China is divided in half. Liu Bang breaks the agreement and pursues Xiang Yu."],
          ["Late 203 or early 202 BC", "The armies of Han surround Xiang Yu at Gaixia."]
        ]},
        "Xiang Yu won almost all the battles of this war. But Liu Bang had something he did not: allies who trusted him and generals he knew how to listen to."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Xiang Yu, about 30 years old" },
        "Tall, very strong, feared in combat, he is remembered in China as the symbol of the tragic hero. He was also cruel: the chroniclers attribute to him massacres of cities and of prisoners, which pushed many people to the side of Han. He distrusted his own advisers and lost the best of them, Fan Zeng.",
        { h: "Liu Bang, about 50 years old" },
        "Son of peasants, a heavy drinker and poorly educated, but generous to those who served him and good at forgiving and recruiting. He lost many battles and always came back. After Gaixia, he would be the emperor Gaozu.",
        { h: "Han Xin" },
        "He started on the Chu side, unnoticed, and went over to Han. In a few years he conquered half of China. At Gaixia, he commanded the bulk of the army, alongside other chiefs allied with Liu Bang."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The 'Records of the Grand Historian', by Sima Qian, written about a century later, says that Han Xin commanded 300,000 men. Estimates for Chu are around 100,000. These are chronicle numbers, probably exaggerated, but everyone agrees that Han had many more people and that the army of Chu was tired and short of food.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "Han Xin put his center in front, with two generals, the marquis of Kong and General Fei, one on each side, and Liu Bang with more troops behind. The center attacked, could not stand the shock of Xiang Yu and fell back. When Chu advanced after it, the two wings closed in from the sides, and the center attacked again. It is a maneuver that recalls Hannibal's at Cannae. Chu was defeated and shut itself in the camp at Gaixia, surrounded on all sides.",
        { h: "Chu songs on all four sides" },
        "At night, Xiang Yu heard songs of his homeland coming from all around the encirclement. According to Sima Qian, he concluded that Han had already conquered all of Chu, because there were so many people from Chu in the enemy army. Many of his soldiers deserted that night.",
        "The chronicle tells that Xiang Yu drank in his tent with his companion, Lady Yu, with his favorite horse, Zhui, nearby, and improvised a poem: he had the strength to uproot mountains, but the times were against him. Lady Yu answered by singing.",
        { note: "Sima Qian records the poem, but the death of Lady Yu that night comes from later texts and operas. It is the plot of the famous Peking opera 'Farewell My Concubine'." },
        { h: "The escape" },
        "That same night, Xiang Yu broke through the encirclement with about 800 horsemen and rode south. At daybreak, 5,000 Han horsemen, commanded by Guan Ying, went after him. Lost in a marsh, deceived by a peasant, he kept losing men until he was left, according to Sima Qian, with 28.",
        "On the bank of the Wu River, a boatman offered to take him east, where he could start over. Xiang Yu refused: he had left there with 8,000 young men and was coming back with none, and he would not have the courage to face their families. He fought on foot against his pursuers and cut his own throat. The reward for his head was divided among five soldiers, each with a piece of the body."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The army of Chu was destroyed; estimates speak of about 80,000 dead and 20,000 captured. The losses of Han are not known. In 202 BC, Liu Bang proclaimed himself emperor.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The Han dynasty lasted, with one interruption, about 400 years, and is seen as the classical age of China. To this day the majority ethnic group of the country is called 'Han', and Chinese characters are 'Han script'.",
        "The expression 'Chu songs on all four sides' became a proverb: to be surrounded, with no support at all.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date: the sources speak of the 12th month of the fifth year of Han, in a calendar that began the year in October. That is why some authors give December 203 BC and others early 202 BC." },
        { note: "The place: tradition puts Gaixia in Lingbi County, in Anhui; there are historians who prefer Guzhen or Luyi." },
        { note: "The fate of Han Xin: the general who gave Liu Bang the victory was accused of treason and executed in 196 BC." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  pidna: {
    war: "Third Macedonian War (171–168 BC)",
    hook: "One hour of fighting, and the kingdom of Alexander ceased to exist.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After the death of Alexander, his empire broke into kingdoms governed by families of generals. In Macedonia reigned the Antigonid dynasty, still with the army that had conquered the world: the [[phalanx|falange]] of long spears.",
        "In the west, Rome had just defeated Carthage and was starting to meddle in Greek affairs. In 197 BC, at Cynoscephalae, the legions defeated King Philip V. Macedonia survived, smaller and watched. Philip's son, Perseus, rebuilt the army and the treasury, and Rome decided to finish him.",
        { h: "The road to Pydna" },
        { timeline: [
          ["197 BC", "Cynoscephalae: Rome defeats Philip V."],
          ["179 BC", "Perseus comes to the throne."],
          ["171 BC", "Rome declares war. Perseus wins the first battle, at Callicinus, asks for peace and is refused."],
          ["171–169 BC", "Three years of weak Roman commanders and campaigns with no result."],
          ["168 BC", "Aemilius Paullus takes over, outflanks the line of the Elpeus River and forces Perseus to fall back to near Pydna."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Lucius Aemilius Paullus, about 60 years old" },
        "Consul for the second time, known for his strict discipline. He was the son of the consul of the same name who died at Cannae, in 216 BC. On arriving, he retrained the army and made the guard shifts harder.",
        { h: "Perseus of Macedonia" },
        "The last king of Macedonia. He had prepared the war well, but the Roman and Greek sources, hostile to him, paint him as greedy and hesitant. Plutarch, citing Polybius, records the accusation that he fled early from the battle, with the excuse of going to offer a sacrifice to Hercules. Another version, attributed to Posidonius, says that he was wounded by a horse's kick and by a javelin and withdrew for that reason.",
        { h: "Scipio Nasica" },
        "Son-in-law of Scipio Africanus. He commanded the force that crossed the passes of Mount Olympus and appeared behind the Macedonian line on the Elpeus River, which forced Perseus to fall back and accept battle."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers vary. The Romans and allies were, according to modern estimates based on Livy, somewhere between 29,000 and 37,000 men, with 22 elephants. Plutarch says that Perseus had almost 40,000 foot soldiers and 4,000 horsemen, with the phalanx in the center, armed with the [[sarissa|sarissa]]. Livy gives about 43,000 for the start of the war, and modern historians calculate far fewer on the day of the battle, near 30,000, because Perseus had detached thousands of men for other tasks. All these numbers come from the ancient sources and should be read as orders of magnitude.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A plain near the coast, by Pydna, cut by a stream, with low hills behind the Roman line. Flat enough for the phalanx at the start, uneven enough for it to fall apart when it advanced.",
        { h: "The night before" },
        "There was an eclipse of the Moon. According to Livy, the tribune Sulpicius Galus had warned the Roman soldiers in advance, and they were not frightened; according to Plutarch, the Macedonians saw the phenomenon as an omen of the fall of their king. The eclipse makes it possible to date the battle: 22 June 168 BC.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "The fighting began in the afternoon, around three o'clock, in a scuffle by the stream, and the sources disagree about how. In Livy, a pack animal escaped and was caught by Thracian soldiers, and the Romans ran after it. Plutarch records two versions: one, that Aemilius Paullus let a horse loose on purpose to provoke the skirmish; another, that the Thracians attacked the Roman pack animals. The skirmish grew, and Perseus ordered the phalanx to advance.",
        "Head-on, the phalanx was terrible. Aemilius Paullus would say later that he had never seen anything so frightening. The Paelignian allies, in front, tried to push aside the spear points and were slaughtered; one commander even threw the standard into the midst of the enemy to force his men to fight. The Roman line fell back to the hills.",
        "But, advancing over the uneven ground and climbing the slope, the phalanx began to open up: some parts moved faster, others stopped, and gaps appeared. Aemilius Paullus sent his [[maniples|manípulo]] into them in small groups.",
        "Inside the formation, the [[sarissa|sarissa]] was useless: it was too long for close fighting. The phalangites had small shields and short swords. The legionaries, with a large shield and the [[gladius|gládio]], made a slaughter. At the same time, on the Roman right wing, the elephants and the allied troops broke the Macedonian left.",
        "The Macedonian cavalry, which hardly fought, withdrew with the king. The phalanx was left alone and was destroyed. According to the sources, it all lasted about an hour."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Livy speaks of up to 20,000 Macedonians dead and about 11,000 captured; Plutarch, of more than 25,000 dead. As for the Romans, Livy speaks of about 100 dead, and Plutarch records 100 according to Posidonius and only 80 according to Scipio Nasica. Historians think the Roman numbers are too low, but the disproportion was real.",
        "Perseus fled to the island of Samothrace, was captured and paraded in the [[triumph|triunfo]] of Aemilius Paullus in Rome, with his children. He spent the rest of his life a prisoner in Italy, at Alba Fucens. Around the time of the triumph, Aemilius Paullus lost his two youngest sons: according to Plutarch, one died five days before, at 14, and the other three days after, at 12.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the end of the dynasty of Alexander and of his Macedonia. Rome divided the kingdom into four republics and, in 146 BC, made it a province. Polybius, a Greek taken to Rome as a hostage after this war, wrote his history precisely to explain how Rome had come to dominate the world in a little more than 50 years.",
        "Militarily, Pydna became the classic example of the flexibility of the [[legion|legião]] against the rigidity of the [[phalanx|falange]]: the phalanx was unbeatable head-on, but it needed flat ground and protected [[flanks|flanco]].",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The start of the battle: the story of the horse let loose on purpose is only one of Plutarch's versions, and modern historians think it more likely that it was a chance clash between Roman soldiers looking for food or water and Thracians." },
        { note: "The treasure: the booty brought from Macedonia was so large that, from 167 BC, Roman citizens stopped paying the direct tax. According to Plutarch, they only started paying it again in the consulship of Hirtius and Pansa, in 43 BC." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  mobei: {
    war: "Han–Xiongnu War, campaign of 119 BC",
    hook: "China went looking for the nomads on the other side of the desert, and came back victorious.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "North of China lay the steppes, and on them the [[Xiongnu|xiongnu]], a confederation of horse peoples. They raided the borders, took people and herds and vanished. In 200 BC, they surrounded the founder of the Han dynasty himself, who escaped only by negotiating. After that, China spent decades sending gifts and princesses to marry the Xiongnu chiefs.",
        "Emperor Wu, who came to the throne in 141 BC, decided to change the policy: instead of paying, attack. He created great cavalry armies and, from 129 BC, sent expeditions deeper and deeper into the steppes.",
        { h: "The road to Mobei" },
        { timeline: [
          ["133 BC", "Failed ambush at Mayi: open war begins."],
          ["129 BC", "First great Han cavalry expeditions. Wei Qing stands out."],
          ["127 BC", "Wei Qing takes the Ordos region, inside the bend of the Yellow River."],
          ["121 BC", "Huo Qubing conquers the Hexi Corridor, the road to the west."],
          ["119 BC", "The Xiongnu fall back to the north of the Gobi. The Han go after them."]
        ]},
        "The name of the battle means 'north of the desert'. In fact there were two separate fights, fought in the same year by the two columns of the Han army."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Wei Qing" },
        "Illegitimate son of a servant, he became the emperor's brother-in-law when his sister, Wei Zifu, was made empress. He proved in war that he was not there only through kinship: he was a methodical and prudent commander.",
        { h: "Huo Qubing, about 20 years old" },
        "Nephew of Wei Qing. Bold and fast, he marched far and lived off what he took from the enemy. He died in 117 BC, at about 23, of illness.",
        { h: "Yizhixie" },
        "The [[chanyu|chanyu]] of the Xiongnu. Following the advice of a defector, Zhao Xin, he fell back to the north of the Gobi, thinking that the Han would not manage to cross the desert or would arrive exhausted.",
        { h: "Li Guang" },
        "A famous veteran, called the 'Flying General' by the Xiongnu, but unlucky. In the campaign of 119 BC, his column got lost in the desert and did not reach the battle."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "According to the Han chronicles, each column had about 50,000 horsemen, supported by a mass of foot soldiers and supply carriers (modern historians speak of more than 100,000) and by many spare horses. For the Xiongnu there is no reliable number: Wei Qing's column is said to have faced about 80,000 horsemen of the chanyu.",
        { note: "The numbers come from the 'Records of the Grand Historian' and the 'Book of Han', official court chronicles. They should be read as orders of magnitude." },
        { h: "The switch of routes" },
        "The plan was for Huo Qubing, with the best cavalry, to attack the chanyu. A prisoner said that the chanyu was in the east, and the routes were swapped: Huo went east, and Wei Qing west. The information was wrong, and it was Wei Qing who found the chanyu.",
        { h: "Wei Qing's battle" },
        "After crossing the desert, Wei Qing found the Xiongnu army waiting. He ordered the armored wagons formed into a ring, like a mobile fort. The charges of the Xiongnu horsemen broke against the wagons, and the fight stalled.",
        "Late in the afternoon, a sandstorm covered the field, and no one could see anyone. Wei Qing took advantage of it to release the cavalry on both sides and envelop the enemy. The chanyu realized he was being surrounded and fled with a few hundred horsemen. The Han pursued the remnants as far as the fortified city of Zhao Xin, where they took the grain that the Xiongnu had stored to feed their troops. They stayed a day and burned the rest before going back.",
        { h: "Huo Qubing's march" },
        "In the east, Huo Qubing crossed the desert, defeated the army of the Wise Prince of the Left, one of the great Xiongnu chiefs, and chased him for hundreds of kilometers. The chronicles speak of more than 70,000 enemies killed or captured. At Mount Langjuxu, probably in the Khentii mountains, in present-day Mongolia, he made sacrifices to heaven, a gesture of taking possession of the land, and went on to a great lake or 'sea' in the north."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Xiongnu lost many men and herds and moved away from the border. But the victory was costly: the 'Records of the Grand Historian' says that, of the roughly 140,000 horses (official and private) counted on leaving the border, fewer than 30,000 came back across it. Li Guang, called to explain why he had been lost, killed himself rather than be tried.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "With the Xiongnu far away, Han China consolidated control of the Hexi Corridor and opened the way to Central Asia, through which the Silk Road would pass. But the cost of the war emptied the treasury and led to new taxes and to the state monopolies on salt and iron.",
        "The Xiongnu kept on existing and, more than a century later, split apart. There are historians who link part of them to the Huns who reached Europe in the 4th century, but this is much debated.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Huo Qubing's 'sea': the sources call it Hanhai. Some think it was Lake Baikal, others a lake in Mongolia or the desert itself. There is no consensus." },
        { note: "Huo Qubing's sentence, on refusing a mansion that the emperor wanted to give him: 'The Xiongnu have not yet been destroyed; what is a house for?'. It is in the 'Records of the Grand Historian', by Sima Qian, and is a free translation of the original." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  carras: {
    war: "Roman–Parthian Wars, campaign of Crassus (54–53 BC)",
    hook: "The richest man in Rome went looking for glory in the desert and found a rain of arrows that never ended.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the 50s BC, Rome was governed in practice by three men, the so-called First Triumvirate: Pompey, the great general; Caesar, who was conquering Gaul; and Marcus Licinius Crassus, the richest man in the city. Crassus was the only one of the three without a great military conquest to show.",
        "East of the Roman province of Syria lay the Parthian Empire, which ran from Mesopotamia to present-day Iran, with an aristocracy that fought on horseback. Rome and Parthia were not at war. Crassus, governor of Syria, decided to attack anyway.",
        { h: "The road to Carrhae" },
        { timeline: [
          ["55 BC", "Crassus is consul for the second time and receives the government of Syria."],
          ["54 BC", "He crosses the Euphrates, occupies cities in Mesopotamia and returns to spend the winter in Syria."],
          ["53 BC", "He rejects the advice of the king of Armenia to go through the mountains and enters by the plain."],
          ["53 BC, early summer", "Near Carrhae, the cavalry of Surena appears before the legions."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Marcus Licinius Crassus, about 62 years old" },
        "He made his fortune buying properties at low prices, even during fires, and lending money to politicians. He had already crushed the revolt of Spartacus, in 71 BC, but the glory of that victory went in large part to Pompey, and Crassus never got over it.",
        { h: "Surena" },
        "It is not a name, it is the title of the family, one of the most powerful in Parthia. According to Plutarch, he was under 30, traveled with a thousand camels of baggage and two hundred wagons of concubines, and was the second man of the kingdom. King Orodes II went with the rest of the army to attack Armenia and left him the task of holding back the Romans.",
        { h: "Publius Crassus and Cassius" },
        "Publius, Crassus's son, had served with Caesar in Gaul and brought a thousand Gallic horsemen. Gaius Cassius Longinus, the quaestor, gave sensible advice that was not heeded. Years later, he would be one of Caesar's assassins.",
        { h: "Abgar" },
        "An Arab chief who guided the Romans along the desert road. Plutarch calls him Ariamnes and treats him as a traitor in the service of the Parthians; Cassius Dio calls him Abgar. In both, he leads Crassus to the open plain, where the Parthian cavalry had the advantage."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Plutarch says that Crassus had seven [[legions|legião]], which gives 28,000 to 35,000 heavy infantry, plus about 4,000 horsemen and as many light soldiers. For Surena, Plutarch counts some 10,000 men, all on horseback, including slaves and vassals; modern reconstructions speak of about 9,000 [[horse archers|arqueiro a cavalo]] and 1,000 [[cataphracts|catafratários]]. His convoy had a thousand camels, and the camels carried spare arrows.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A dry, open plain near the city of Carrhae, today Harran, in southern Turkey. Perfect for cavalry; terrible for infantry, which had nowhere to take cover.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "Cassius suggested forming a long line, so as not to be surrounded. Crassus began that way and then changed to a large square, with cavalry on the sides. Surena had hidden the armor under cloaks; suddenly, to the sound of drums, the cataphracts revealed themselves, shining in the sun. But they did not charge: the square was too solid.",
        "Then the horse archers surrounded the square and began to shoot. According to Plutarch, the arrows went through shields and pinned a soldier's hand to the wood. When the Romans advanced, the Parthians fell back shooting backward, the [[Parthian shot|tiro parta]]. When the Romans stood still, in [[testudo|testudo]], they became a fixed target.",
        "The Romans hoped that the arrows would run out. That was when they saw the camels arriving to refill the quivers.",
        "Crassus ordered his son Publius to attack to relieve the pressure, with about 1,300 horsemen, 500 archers and 8 [[cohorts|coorte]]. The Parthians pretended to flee, drew Publius far away and surrounded him. The Gallic horsemen fought bravely, but they had light armor against the cataphracts. Publius, wounded, asked a shield-bearer to kill him.",
        "The Parthians came back with Publius's head stuck on a lance and showed it to his father. The attack resumed until dark.",
        { h: "The retreat" },
        "At night, the Parthians moved away. The Romans fled to Carrhae, leaving behind about 4,000 wounded, who were killed the next day. Days later, trying to reach the mountains of Armenia, Crassus agreed to go and negotiate with Surena, pressed by his own soldiers. In the talk there was a scuffle, and he was killed."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "According to Plutarch, 20,000 Romans died and 10,000 were captured. Cassius escaped with about 500 horsemen. The Parthians took the eagles, the sacred standards of the legions, an enormous humiliation for Rome.",
        "Plutarch tells that the head and right hand of Crassus reached King Orodes, in Armenia, during a banquet at which Euripides's 'The Bacchae' was being performed, and that an actor used the head on stage in place of Pentheus's. Surena did not make use of the victory: King Orodes, suspicious, had him killed shortly after.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "With the death of Crassus, the triumvirate became a direct confrontation between Caesar and Pompey, which ended in civil war four years later. And Rome learned that in the east there was an empire capable of beating it. The Euphrates frontier was disputed for centuries, first with the Parthians, then with the Sasanian Persians.",
        "The eagles only returned to Rome in 20 BC, through a diplomatic agreement by Augustus, who celebrated the fact as a victory. The scene appears on the breastplate of the famous statue of Augustus of Prima Porta.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The prisoners: Pliny says that the captives were taken to Merv, in present-day Turkmenistan. A popular theory claims that some ended up in China, in a village called Liqian. Genetic tests did not confirm it, and most historians consider it a legend." },
        { note: "The sources: the main account is the 'Life of Crassus', by Plutarch, written some 150 years later and very dramatic. The numbers and several scenes are hard to verify." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  alesia: {
    war: "Gallic Wars (58–50 BC), revolt of Vercingetorix (52 BC)",
    hook: "A Roman army trapped between two walls, attacked from inside and from outside at the same time.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "From 58 BC, Julius Caesar, a Roman governor, went on conquering Gaul, more or less today's France and Belgium, tribe by tribe. The Gauls were dozens of independent peoples, often enemies of one another, and Caesar used these rivalries to his advantage.",
        "In the winter of 53 to 52 BC, with Caesar in Italy, Gaul rose up. A young noble of the Arverni people, Vercingetorix, managed something unheard of: to unite most of the tribes under a single command.",
        { h: "The road to Alesia" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Early 52 BC", "General revolt. Vercingetorix is chosen supreme chief."],
          ["Spring 52 BC", "Caesar takes Avaricum (Bourges) and massacres almost the whole population."],
          ["Summer 52 BC", "Caesar is defeated while attacking Gergovia, the city of the Arverni."],
          ["Late summer 52 BC", "A cavalry battle goes badly for the Gauls, and Vercingetorix shuts himself in Alesia."],
          ["September 52 BC", "The Gallic relief army arrives. Final battle."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Julius Caesar, about 48 years old" },
        "An ambitious politician in debt, who used Gaul to gain money, an army and fame. He himself wrote the history of the war, in the 'Commentaries on the Gallic War', the main source on Alesia. It is the winner's version.",
        { h: "Vercingetorix, about 30 years old" },
        "Son of an Arvernian chief executed for wanting to be king. He used scorched earth: burning villages and granaries so that the Romans would have nothing to eat. At Alesia, he bet that the relief of all Gaul would crush the besiegers.",
        { h: "The relief commanders" },
        "Commius, of the Atrebates, a former ally of Caesar, and Vercassivellaunus, cousin of Vercingetorix, among others. They commanded the enormous army that came to free Alesia."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Caesar had 10 or 11 legions, plus auxiliaries and Germanic cavalry: somewhere between 60,000 and 75,000 men, according to modern estimates. Inside Alesia, according to him, there were 80,000 Gauls. The relief army is said to have had 250,000 foot soldiers and 8,000 horsemen. Historians think these figures of Caesar's are greatly exaggerated, but accept that the Romans were outnumbered.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Alesia stood on top of a hill with steep sides, Mont Auxois, between two rivers and surrounded by other hills. Attacking head-on would be suicide. Caesar decided to win by hunger.",
        { h: "The two rings" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the Romans built the [[circumvallation|circunvalação]], a line of about 11 Roman miles (some 16 km) around the city, with ditches, an earth wall with a palisade, and towers. Before it closed, Vercingetorix sent his cavalry out at night to seek relief throughout Gaul.",
        "Knowing this, Caesar ordered a second line built, facing outward, the [[contravallation|contravalação]], of 14 miles (about 21 km). In front of the lines, traps: sharpened branches buried in the ground, holes with hidden stakes, which the soldiers called 'lilies', and iron hooks fixed in blocks of wood. The Romans were left in the middle, like in a doughnut.",
        "Inside the city, the food ran out. The Gauls drove out the women, the children and the old people of the local people, the Mandubii. Caesar would not let them through, and they were left stuck between the lines.",
        { h: "The attacks" },
        "The relief army arrived and camped on the hills outside. First there was a great cavalry battle on the plain, won by the Romans thanks to the Germanic horsemen. Then, a night attack from both sides at the same time, which the traps and the artillery stopped.",
        "The Roman weak point was a camp at the foot of a hill, to the north, which the fortification could not cover well. Vercassivellaunus hid about 60,000 picked men there and attacked at noon, while the rest of the relief army advanced at other points and Vercingetorix attacked from inside.",
        "Caesar sent Labienus with six cohorts to reinforce the point in danger, and later more troops. He went in person from one point to another, recognizable by his red cloak. Finally, he sent part of the cavalry out of the lines to go around the outside, and it fell on the backs of the Gauls. The relief army panicked and fell apart. Vercassivellaunus was captured."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The next day, Vercingetorix surrendered. Plutarch describes a theatrical scene: he came out mounted and armed, rode a circle around Caesar, dismounted and threw his weapons at his feet. Caesar says only that the chiefs were handed over. Vercingetorix was kept prisoner for about six years, paraded in Caesar's [[triumph|triunfo]] in 46 BC and was executed.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the end of organized Gallic resistance. Gaul became Roman, adopted Latin, from which French would come, and stayed Roman for five centuries. For Caesar, the victory brought money, loyal soldiers and prestige, everything he would use to cross the Rubicon three years later.",
        "Militarily, the two lines of Alesia are one of the most studied feats of field engineering of Antiquity.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The place: in the 19th century, Napoleon III had Alise-Sainte-Reine, in Burgundy, excavated and raised a great statue of Vercingetorix there. Others have defended Chaux-des-Crotenay, in the Jura. The Franco-German excavations of the 1990s found ditches, weapons and coins that, for the great majority of archaeologists, confirm Alise." },
        { note: "The numbers: the 250,000 of the relief army come from Caesar, who had every interest in enlarging the victory. Many historians think the real figure was much smaller." },
        { note: "The national hero: Vercingetorix was almost forgotten for centuries and became a symbol of France in the 19th century. The comic 'Asterix' plays with this: the Gauls of the village pretend not to know where Alesia is." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  farsalia: {
    war: "Caesar's Civil War (49–45 BC)",
    hook: "Six cohorts hidden behind a line decided who would rule Rome.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "With the death of Crassus at Carrhae, in 53 BC, two strongmen were left in Rome: Caesar, who had just conquered Gaul, and Pompey, the most celebrated general of his generation. The Senate, afraid of Caesar, allied itself with Pompey and ordered Caesar to hand over his army.",
        "In January 49 BC, Caesar crossed the Rubicon River with one legion, the border of his province, which was an act of war. Pompey and a good part of the Senate left Italy and went to Greece, where there was money, allies and time to raise an army.",
        { h: "The road to Pharsalus" },
        { timeline: [
          ["January 49 BC", "Caesar crosses the Rubicon. Pompey leaves Italy."],
          ["49 BC", "Caesar defeats Pompey's armies in Hispania, at Ilerda."],
          ["Early 48 BC", "Caesar crosses the Adriatic, despite the enemy fleet."],
          ["July 48 BC", "At Dyrrhachium, Caesar tries to surround Pompey and suffers a serious defeat."],
          ["August 48 BC", "The two armies meet in Thessaly, near Pharsalus."]
        ]},
        "After Dyrrhachium, according to Plutarch and Suetonius, Caesar said that the enemy would have won the war that day if they had had a leader who knew how to win. Pompey did not pursue, and Caesar withdrew into the interior of Greece to feed his troops."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Julius Caesar, 52 years old" },
        "He came from eight years of war in Gaul, with veteran legions that adored him. He was quick, took great risks and knew how to read the enemy. He is also the author of the main source on the battle, the 'Civil War'.",
        { h: "Pompey the Great, 57 years old" },
        "Victor over the pirates, over Mithridates and over half of the East, called 'the Great' since he was young. He preferred to beat Caesar by hunger and weariness, without risking a battle. But the senators in his camp, already squabbling over the posts of the postwar period, pressed him to fight.",
        { h: "Labienus" },
        "Titus Labienus had been Caesar's right-hand man in Gaul and went over to Pompey's side. He commanded the large Pompeian cavalry and knew his old chief's methods closely."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "According to Caesar, he had 22,000 legionaries and 1,000 horsemen; Pompey, 45,000 legionaries and 7,000 horsemen. Modern historians suspect that Caesar inflated the enemy, but accept that Pompey had about twice the infantry and a much larger cavalry.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A plain in Thessaly, by the Enipeus River. Pompey's right and Caesar's left rested on the river, which protected that side. The other end, open, was the obvious place for the cavalry.",
        { h: "The plans" },
        { formation: true },
        "Pompey's plan was simple: hold Caesar's infantry head-on and hurl all of Labienus's cavalry, with archers and slingers, against Caesar's right wing, go around it and attack from behind.",
        "Caesar saw the cavalry all concentrated on one side and understood the plan. He took one [[cohort|coorte]] from each legion of the third line, six in all, something like 2,000 to 3,000 men, and formed with them a fourth line, behind his right, where the enemy cavalry could not see them. According to Plutarch, he ordered them not to throw the [[pilum|pilo]], but to use it as a spear, aiming at the faces of the horsemen.",
        "He also ordered that the third line should not enter combat without an order. It would be the [[reserve|reserva]] for the end.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "Pompey ordered his infantry to wait standing still, so that Caesar's men would arrive tired. Caesar's veterans noticed, stopped halfway, caught their breath and only then attacked. Caesar criticized Pompey's order afterward: for him, the momentum of the run heated up the soldiers.",
        "Labienus's cavalry charged, pushed back Caesar's small cavalry and began to go around. That was when the six hidden cohorts advanced. The horsemen, who did not expect to find infantry there, fell back in panic and fled to the hills. The archers and slingers, unprotected, were slaughtered.",
        "The cohorts kept going and fell on Pompey's left [[flank|flanco]]. At that moment, Caesar launched the third line, still rested. The Pompeian infantry gave way.",
        "Pompey, according to Caesar and Plutarch, went back to the camp hardly saying anything. When the Caesarians invaded it, he took off his general's insignia and fled on horseback."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Caesar says he lost about 200 soldiers and 30 centurions, and that the Pompeians had about 15,000 dead and more than 24,000 prisoners. Asinius Pollio, a Caesarian officer who also wrote about the war, speaks of 6,000 Pompeian dead, according to Plutarch, and Appian gives up to 1,200 dead on Caesar's side. Caesar pardoned most of the prisoners, among them Marcus Brutus.",
        "According to Suetonius, who cites Asinius Pollio, on seeing the enemy dead on the field Caesar is said to have said: 'They wanted it this way'.",
        "Pompey fled to Egypt, hoping for the help of the young king Ptolemy XIII. The king's advisers preferred to please the victor: they killed Pompey as he landed, in September 48 BC. When Pompey's head and ring were brought to him, Caesar, according to Plutarch, turned his face away and wept.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The war still lasted until 45 BC, in Africa and Hispania, but Pharsalus decided the essential. Caesar became dictator and, in 44 BC, was assassinated. From the new civil war would come Augustus, the first emperor. The Roman Republic never came back.",
        "Tactically, it is a famous example of a hidden reserve used against a maneuver that the commander guessed in advance.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The first to attack: Caesar tells that the veteran Crastinus promised him that, alive or dead, he would earn his thanks that day, and led the charge. He died from a sword thrust in the face." },
        { note: "The date: 9 August 48 BC in the Roman calendar of the time, which was out of sync with the seasons. Corrected for the seasons, the day would fall about two months earlier, around June." },
        { note: "The sources: almost everything comes from Caesar, a brilliant and interested author. The poet Lucan wrote an epic poem about the war, the 'Pharsalia', from the point of view of the defeated." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

